# .bkit/.env — local secrets for MCP servers and agent tooling, shipped by bkit.
# <!-- bkit:managed v5.4.0 — do not edit; run `bkit upgrade` to refresh -->
#
# Copy this file to .bkit/.env (gitignored), fill in your values, then run:
#   direnv allow
# direnv will auto-export these into your shell whenever you cd into this repo.
#
# Cloud sessions (Claude on the web, Cursor Background Agents) set the SAME
# variable names in their respective dashboards — never in this file.
#
# Keep ~/.config/bkit/launchd.env in sync. GUI/daemon apps (the opencode desktop
# app, Cursor launched from the Dock, and opencode's background service) do not
# inherit direnv's exports — they read what `ai.bkit.launchd` publishes to launchd
# from that file (see .bkit/providers.md § macOS GUI-isolation). It DUPLICATES
# these tokens and does not track this file, so after ROTATING a value here you
# must re-sync launchd.env or the app keeps the stale one — e.g. a revoked
# BKIT_GH_TOKEN makes the GitHub MCP 401 and opencode offers an OAuth "sign in"
# that then fails with "dynamic auth not supported". Then restart long-lived
# consumers: opencode snapshots its env at launch, so `opencode service restart`.

# Optional examples — not required bkit tooling. Listed so direnv has a name to
# load if you use that surface; leave blank otherwise.

# BKIT_GH_TOKEN — the general-purpose bkit GitHub token. One token for every GitHub
# *API* operation bkit tooling performs:
#   - the GitHub MCP server (api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/) via the Authorization header
#     in .mcp.json / .cursor/mcp.json / .opencode/opencode.json;
#   - self-hosted Renovate's PLATFORM auth (repo Actions secret) when you copy
#     the example `.github/workflows/bkit-renovate.yml` — opens PRs,
#     pushes renovate/* branches, edits workflow SHAs, maintains the dashboard issue.
#
# Token TYPE — choose by how many repos / owners bkit touches:
#   - ONE repo, or every repo under ONE owner (a single user, or a single org) →
#     FINE-GRAINED PAT (resource owner = that user or org). Preferred — repo-scoped
#     blast radius + mandatory expiry. Permissions below.
#   - MORE THAN ONE repo across DIFFERENT owners (two or more orgs, or a user + an org) →
#     CLASSIC PAT. A fine-grained PAT is limited to a SINGLE resource owner and cannot
#     span organizations, so it cannot cover this case. Classic reaches every repo your
#     user can access — keep the expiry short; an org may block classic PATs (403), and
#     SAML SSO orgs need the token authorized per org (authorization expires; re-authorize).
#
# FINE-GRAINED PAT — Repository permissions (as labelled in the token UI; scope to the
# repos you work on): Contents: Read and write · Pull requests: Read and write · Issues:
# Read and write · Workflows: Read and write · Commit statuses: Read and write (Renovate
# reads a commit's CI state to open the PR + posts its own stability checks; without it it
# creates the renovate/* branch but 403s on /commits/{sha}/statuses and never opens the PR)
# · Dependabot alerts: Read (GitHub's vulnerability feed, for security-fix PRs) · Metadata:
# Read-only. (Actions: Read for the MCP server's run-status queries.) Add Organization
# Projects: Read and write only for board sync (org-owned boards; shown under the token's
# Organizations tab, not Repository permissions). There is NO "Checks" permission on
# fine-grained PATs — check-run writes are App-only, so a PAT-based Renovate uses commit
# statuses instead.
# CLASSIC PAT — scopes: `repo` (issues / PRs / contents / commit statuses) · `workflow`
# · `read:org` · `project` (board sync) · `security_events` (Dependabot / code-scanning
# feed) · optional `user:email` (Renovate commit authorship on a personal account). There
# is no separate Checks scope on classic either — commit statuses come via `repo`.
# Self-hosted Renovate commit authorship: on a PERSONAL-account fine-grained token also add
# the Account permission "Email addresses: Read" so commits use your verified email.
# ORG-owned tokens have NO Email-addresses permission — instead set the repo variable
# RENOVATE_GIT_AUTHOR (Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions -> Variables), e.g.
# `Renovate Bot <renovate@your-domain>` (classic equivalent: the `user:email` scope).
# This is a WRITE token — do NOT put it on read-only surfaces (Vercel env / project
# .npmrc).
# Generate at: https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens (fine-grained)
#              https://github.com/settings/tokens (classic)
BKIT_GH_TOKEN=

# BKIT_LINEAR_API_KEY — Linear personal API key. Used by the Linear MCP server
# (mcp.linear.app/mcp) and by the Linear preflight hook in
# .claude/hooks/bkit-preflight.sh and .cursor/hooks/bkit-preflight.sh.
# Generate at: https://linear.app/settings/api
BKIT_LINEAR_API_KEY=

# BKIT_JIRA_API_TOKEN — Atlassian API token for the Jira MCP server
# (mcp.atlassian.com/v2/mcp), sent as `Authorization: Bearer`. Create it with
# "Create API token with scopes" and pick the app "Atlassian MCP V2"; a classic
# token or another app is refused. An org admin must first allow API-token sign-in
# for the Rovo MCP server. Steps: .bkit/trackers.md § jira.
# Generate at: https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens
BKIT_JIRA_API_TOKEN=

# BKIT_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY — Anthropic API key. Only needed if you run Claude Code
# without a Claude subscription (pay-per-token mode). Subscription users leave
# blank.
# Generate at: https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
BKIT_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# BKIT_CURSOR_API_KEY — Cursor API key. Only needed if you dispatch Cursor cloud
# agents from this shell (programmatic POST /v0/agents, model catalog queries
# via `curl https://api.cursor.com/v0/models -u "$BKIT_CURSOR_API_KEY:"`). The
# Cursor desktop IDE does NOT use this — it authenticates via its own login.
# Generate at: https://cursor.com/settings → API Keys
BKIT_CURSOR_API_KEY=

# BKIT_OPENCODE_API_KEY — OpenCode gateway API key (one key covers both plans). Used with
# `bkit install --opencode=opencode-zen` or `--opencode=opencode-go`. Zen is the full gateway
# (https://opencode.ai/zen/v1; ids `opencode-zen/<id>`); Go is the budget open-weight-only plan
# (https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1; ids `opencode-go/<id>`). Default Go pins: minimax-m3 (opus),
# qwen3.7-plus (sonnet), deepseek-v4-pro/-flash — prefer pins that stay within the Go 5h usage window.
# Generate at: https://opencode.ai/auth (sign in → Create API Key).
BKIT_OPENCODE_API_KEY=

# BKIT_OPENROUTER_API_KEY — OpenRouter API key. Used with `bkit install --opencode=openrouter`.
# One key → every model (incl. :free variants) via openrouter.ai/api/v1. Key format: sk-or-…
# Generate at: https://openrouter.ai/keys
BKIT_OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# BKIT_OLLAMA_API_KEY — Ollama API key. Used with `bkit install --opencode=ollama`
# (ollama.com/v1). Generate at: https://ollama.com/settings/keys
BKIT_OLLAMA_API_KEY=

# BKIT_TAVILY_API_KEY — preferred example for the `context=web` context-fetch block in
# .agents/context.md. Swap that block for any search API that returns url + snippet;
# rename this key to match. No key → the example self-skips. Cloud: same name in
# the dashboard. Generate at: https://app.tavily.com
BKIT_TAVILY_API_KEY=

# Add your own secrets below. This file is what direnv exports into every local
# agent shell (`dotenv_if_exists .bkit/.env` in `.envrc`) and what `bkit install`
# copies into the cloud dashboard paste block — so a key here reaches `/bkit-debug`
# (and any other agent bash) locally, and the same name is waiting in the dashboard
# for headless runs. App env (`.env.local`, Vercel/Supabase) is NOT on that path
# unless you append another `dotenv_if_exists` in `.envrc`. Gitignored; never commit.
